  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Hands Off II Angola</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Brief overall description:
To reduce violence against sex workers, Aidsfonds works together with sex worker-led groups ADM and ATSA. They will build a strong national movement and will provide prevention, care and support services. Human rights violations against sex workers will be documented and used for ladvocacy. The organisations will advocate for law reform and policy and service provision changes.   Relationships with key stakeholders, such as service providers, community and religious leaders and police officers will be sought or intensified to reduce stigma and discrimination. Organisation ASSC will also support with outreach to sex workers. 

Main objectives and outcomes:
Hands Off II partners work to reduce violence against sex workers at community, national and regional level. Outcomes the organisations work on mainly include: an empowered and resilient sex worker movement demands its rights; increased access to and use of inclusive services for sex workers; and a more enabling and supportive environment for sex work.

Description of target groups: 
The programme&#x2019;s primary target group is female, male and transgender sex workers, meaning those who receive money or goods in exchange for sexual services, either regularly or occasionally. The organisations will also work with lawyers, government officials, policymakers and service providers.

Additional information:
 The legal status of sex work in Angola is ambiguous; while it is not explicitly criminalized, related activities such as brothel-keeping and pimping are illegal, which creates a precarious environment for sex workers. 
Sex work in Angola involves a significant and vulnerable population, estimated at around 35,000 individuals. These sex workers face numerous challenges, including widespread stigma, discrimination, and violence, often perpetrated by clients and law enforcement officials. Health issues, particularly a high prevalence of HIV, are also a major concern, exacerbated by limited access to healthcare and social services.</narrative>
  </description>
